{
  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The text is only a bare notice that the Small Business Committee chair held a markup on pending venture capital and education bills. Under negative clarification (2), holding markups on pending legislation without any claimed delivered benefit is not credit-claiming. There is no substantive content tying the senator to any secured benefit."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's statement at a Judiciary Committee hearing advocates for cameras in federal courtrooms, criticizes Bush administration secrecy, and notes he has cosponsored pending bills (Sunshine in the Courtroom Act, Televising Supreme Court Proceedings Act). Under negative clarification (1) and (2), this is position taking and cosponsorship of pending legislation; no benefit is claimed as delivered."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter to the Acting Secretary of the Air Force requesting that clear religious expression guidelines be developed for the Air Force Academy. Under negative clarification (2) and (5), a letter urging an agency to act is advocacy with no claimed delivered benefit; he expresses hopes for draft guidelines but nothing has been secured."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline itself ('Medicare Penalty to Be Waived for Hurricane Affected Seniors... Administrator finally responds to Sen Landrieu's letter') presents an agency action — waiving the Medicare Part D late-enrollment penalty for 250,000+ Louisiana seniors — as a direct response to Landrieu's letter and pressure. Clarification (3) explicitly names 'penalty waivers' following the senator's letter as credit-claiming. This mirrors the Lott positive example where an agency action followed the senator's appeal."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Landrieu and Kerry 'call for' local contracts in Katrina/Rita rebuilding and write the Small Business Administration to speed up loss verification. Under negative clarification (2) and (5), urging an agency to act and writing letters where no benefit has been secured is advocacy, not credit-claiming. No delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "DeWine announces a $78,046 federal grant to the Troy Township (Ohio) Fire Department delivered through the FIRE Act, which the release says he 'wrote and passed,' and notes he 'helped secure $650 million' for the program. This combines clarification (1) (announcement of a federal grant to state constituents), (2) (explicit securing verbs), and (5) (claims of past delivered accomplishments — a law he authored now in force). It matches the Talent positive example of announcing funds flowing to the state."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Coleman's statement extends sympathies on the death of Rep. Charlie Norwood and expresses hope that Congress will pass his resolution. Under negative clarification (3), condolences are not credit-claiming, and hoping for future passage of a resolution claims no delivered benefit."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's remarks at the CHIP enrollment ceremony urge the President not to veto the bill and credit Baucus, Rockefeller, Grassley, and Hatch — not himself — for the bipartisan effort. The bill's benefits are threatened and not yet delivered (veto pending), and Reid claims no personal role in securing them. Under negative clarification (1) and (5), this is position taking and advocacy directed at the President, plus criticism of the administration."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the release concerns seeking an expedited hearing on pending legislation (which alone would be advocacy), the final paragraph presents a delivered benefit: after Domenici and the New Mexico House members asked the Agriculture Secretary and the President to approve emergency aid, the USDA Farm Service Agency announced that livestock producers in 20 New Mexico counties qualify for low-interest emergency loans. Under clarification (1) and (3), an announcement of disaster-related aid eligibility presented as following the senator's request is credit-claiming, analogous to the Lott trailers example."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig announces that the VA will expand clinic services in Lewiston, Idaho (from two days per month to five days per week plus mental health services), and frames the decision as VA officials responding to 'my concerns.' Clarification (3) explicitly includes 'service expansions' following the senator's advocacy, and clarification (1) covers announcements of federal actions benefiting state constituents. A concrete benefit to identifiable beneficiaries (Idaho veterans) is presented as delivered."
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release mainly announces the introduction of the CARES Act (which alone would not qualify under negative clarification (2)), it also reports a completed intervention: 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape, Sen Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan, a little girl from Iraq with life threatening birth defects, was able to come... to the United States for treatment.' Clarification (5) includes casework-style interventions that produced results, so the release contains a claim of a delivered benefit."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding to control coqui frogs on the Big Island and explicitly claims personal responsibility: after OMB denied Hawaii's request, 'I then went looking and found another federal funding source to at least begin the control and containment process.' This is a first-person securing claim tied to funds flowing to Hawaii, satisfying the core test and clarifications (1) and (2)."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman's National Review op-ed reflects on the 9/11 anniversary and argues for staying the course in Iraq. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure op-ed style position taking; no benefit is delivered and no personal accomplishment is claimed."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "McCaskill announces Senate passage of the Improving America's Security Act, touts $3 billion for first responders plus an estimated $26.5 million for Missouri, and highlights that 'the bill also contains McCaskill's amendment' providing whistleblower protection and bargaining rights for airport screeners. Under clarification (4), the senator's amendment adopted in a bill that has passed the chamber, with benefits to identifiable beneficiaries touted, is credit-claiming; the boundary rule treats an adopted/passed measure as credit claiming rather than position taking."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement at a Commerce Committee hearing expresses support for Vice Admiral Allen's nomination as Coast Guard Commandant and praises the Coast Guard's performance. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position taking on a nomination; no benefit to Maine or any constituency is claimed as delivered, and Snowe claims no personal accomplishment."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Graham announces Senate passage (79-14) of the Farm Bill and claims personal credit: 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs' benefiting South Carolina research and training, and the bill 'includes a Graham backed provision' providing 4-cents-per-pound assistance to textile manufacturers. The state agriculture commissioner thanks him for 'securing passage.' Under clarification (4), the senator's provisions included in a measure that passed the chamber, with benefits to identifiable beneficiaries touted, is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Reed announces Senate passage (52-40) of the FY2008 budget resolution, which he supported, and specifically notes it includes 'a provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund,' while touting benefits for children's health insurance, veterans, and Pell Grants. Under clarification (4), a provision personally championed by the senator that is included in a measure passed by the chamber, with benefits to identifiable beneficiaries touted, meets the credit-claiming test. Some ambiguity exists because a budget resolution is a non-binding blueprint and the housing fund is not yet created, but the codebook's boundary rule treats adopted/passed measures as credit claiming."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions praises TVA's board for voting to proceed with a nuclear license application at Bellefonte. Although he says putting Bellefonte to good use 'has been a priority of mine,' he claims no causal role in the decision — he 'applaud[s] the Board.' Under negative clarification (5), welcoming or applauding someone else's decision without claiming a causal role is not credit-claiming; stating a priority is not a claim of having secured a benefit."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker announce that the Department of Transportation will support new non-stop air service between Jackson, TN and Cincinnati under the Essential Air Service program, and the release notes the senators 'wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application.' Under clarification (3), agency actions presented as following the senator's letter — explicitly including 'service expansions' — are credit-claiming, as is announcing a federal benefit flowing to the state under clarification (1)."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release centers on Domenici cosponsoring the newly introduced R-HoPE Act (which alone would be position taking under negative clarification (2)), it also claims a past delivered accomplishment: the Physician Fee Schedule Work Geographic Adjustment provisions 'that Domenici first authored' were included in the Medicare Modernization Act and are current law, putting rural physicians 'more on par with urban doctors in terms of Medicare reimbursement levels.' Clarification (5) includes claims of past delivered accomplishments — laws authored now in force — so the release contains a personal credit claim for an existing benefit. I acknowledge some ambiguity since the dominant frame is a bill introduction."
  }
}
